from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('10');add=A.add
add('2.10.3-01','Energy is conserved across a complete system and its surroundings.','A decrease in one energy store is balanced by increases elsewhere or by transfers across the chosen boundary.','A pendulum exchanges gravitational potential and kinetic energy. At the highest points it has maximum gravitational energy and momentarily zero speed; near the bottom its speed and kinetic energy are greatest. If air resistance and pivot friction are negligible, the sum of these two stores stays constant. With resistance, the mechanical sum falls while internal energy of the surroundings rises. Define the boundary before deciding whether energy has been lost from a particular store.','Explain why a real pendulum gradually reaches a lower height without energy being destroyed.','Air resistance and pivot friction transfer mechanical energy to internal energy.~The mechanical store falls, but energy is conserved when surroundings are included.')
add('2.10.3-02','Doing mechanical work transfers energy through a force acting over a displacement.','For a constant force parallel to motion, transferred energy is Fd.','Pulling a trolley with 7 N over 3 m transfers 21 J through that force. This is not necessarily its gain in kinetic energy: other forces may transfer energy to internal stores. At steady speed, kinetic energy is unchanged even though the pulling force does positive work. Distinguish the work by one force from net work by the resultant force.','Calculate the energy transferred by a constant 8 N force acting along a 2.5 m displacement.','W = Fd = 8 × 2.5.~The transfer is 20 J.')
add('2.10.3-03','Kinetic energy is associated with motion; potential energy is associated with position or configuration.','Gravitational and strain energy are different potential energy stores.','A raised load stores gravitational energy relative to a chosen reference height. A stretched spring stores strain energy because of its deformation. Releasing either can produce motion and kinetic energy. More than one store may be present at once: an object falling from a height generally has both gravitational potential and kinetic energy before it reaches the reference level.','Explain the energy-store link when a stretched spring launches a small trolley horizontally.','Combine the stores: the deformed spring initially has strain potential energy.~As it relaxes, energy is transferred into the trolley’s kinetic store, with some transfer to the surroundings.')
add('2.10.3-03-M01','Kinetic energy is the energy of motion.','For a given mass, increasing speed increases kinetic energy.','The relationship Ek = ½mv² shows that doubling speed multiplies kinetic energy by four, rather than two. The speed is squared, so kinetic energy is nonnegative and does not record motion direction. A moving trolley can transfer its kinetic energy by doing work on a resisting object.','Explain why a faster trolley of the same mass can do more work while stopping.','Its greater speed gives it a larger kinetic energy store.~That store can transfer more energy through work during stopping.')
add('2.10.3-03-M02','Gravitational potential energy depends on position in a gravitational field.','Near Earth’s surface, raising a mass through a height increases its gravitational store by mgh.','Height is measured vertically, not along a sloping path. A long gentle ramp and a short steep ramp reaching the same height give the same gravitational energy gain for the same mass in the same field. The work input may differ when friction is included. The reference level sets the quoted store, while a change in height sets its change.','Explain why raising the same load to the same vertical height gives the same gravitational energy gain using two frictionless ramps.','The change is mgh and the mass, field strength and vertical height are equal.~Ramp length changes the required force and distance, not that energy gain.')
add('2.10.3-03-M03','Strain potential energy is stored when a body is elastically deformed.','It can be released when the body returns toward its original shape.','A stretched spring and a compressed spring can both store strain energy. Stretching beyond the elastic range can leave permanent deformation and dissipate energy. Do not equate deformation length directly with energy: work depends on the force acting throughout the deformation. This topic uses the qualitative store and its transfers rather than assuming an unstated spring-energy formula.','Explain how a compressed spring can produce motion when released.','Compression stores strain potential energy.~As the spring expands, it can do work and transfer energy into a moving object’s kinetic store.')
add('2.10.3-04','Near a uniform gravitational field, gravitational energy change is mgh and kinetic energy is ½mv².','Equating an energy decrease with an increase requires accounting for other transfers.','For an object released from rest through height h with negligible resistance, mgh = ½mv². The mass cancels, giving v² = 2gh. If drag is appreciable, some gravitational energy is transferred to internal energy and the kinetic gain is smaller. Do not equate the two stores merely because both formulas are available.','Explain when mgh may be equated to ½mv² for a falling object.','Link the gravitational decrease to the kinetic increase for release from rest.~This equality requires negligible other transfers, such as air resistance.',heading='Using kinetic and gravitational energy equations')
add('2.10.3-04-M01','A near-surface gravitational energy change is ΔEp = mgΔh.','Use kilograms, N/kg and metres for an answer in joules.','A 3 kg load raised by 1.2 m where g = 10 N/kg gains 36 J. A downward displacement gives a decrease in the gravitational store. State whether reporting the signed change or its magnitude. The same school formula assumes the field does not vary appreciably over the height.','Calculate the gravitational energy gained by 2 kg raised 1.5 m where g = 10 N/kg.','ΔEp = mgΔh = 2 × 10 × 1.5.~The gain is 30 J.')
add('2.10.3-04-M02','Kinetic energy is Ek = ½mv².','Use mass in kg and speed in m/s to obtain joules.','For 4 kg moving at 3 m/s, square the speed first: Ek = ½ × 4 × 9 = 18 J. When finding speed from energy, rearrange v² = 2Ek/m and take the square root. A negative speed squared does not imply negative energy; kinetic energy uses speed rather than signed velocity.','Calculate the kinetic energy of a 0.50 kg object moving at 4 m/s.','Ek = ½mv² = 0.5 × 0.50 × 16.~The kinetic energy is 4 J.')
add('2.10.4-01','Work done by a constant force is force multiplied by displacement in the force direction.','The displacement component, rather than the lever arm, is relevant.','A force perpendicular to motion does no work through that displacement. Holding a stationary bag does no mechanical work on the bag during the hold because its displacement is zero, though the person uses energy internally. For an opposing force, energy is transferred out of the moving object’s mechanical store. These distinctions keep work separate from everyday meanings of effort.','Define work done by a constant force.','It is the force multiplied by the displacement in the direction of that force.',heading='Work done')
add('2.10.4-02','For parallel constant force and displacement, W = Fd.','Rearrange as F = W/d or d = W/F when the physical conditions fit.','If 48 J is transferred by a constant 12 N force along the motion, the displacement is 4 m. Use the force responsible for the stated transfer. An effort force and a resultant force need not be equal; using the resultant computes net mechanical work, while using one applied force computes that force’s contribution.','Calculate the displacement along a 6 N constant force when it does 18 J of work.','d = W/F = 18/6.~The displacement is 3 m.')
add('2.10.4-03','Energy is the capacity to do work in the syllabus description.','Both energy and work are measured in joules.','A battery, raised load and moving object have different energy stores but can each transfer energy to other systems. Work describes one transfer mechanism, rather than an additional substance stored in a body. Use the same joule unit when comparing an energy decrease with work done.','State the SI unit shared by energy and work.','The joule, J.')
add('2.10.4-04','One joule equals one newton metre of work.','It is the work done by 1 N acting over 1 m in its direction.','Keep both quantities in SI units before combining them. A force of 1 N moving its application point through 1 cm does 0.01 J, not 1 J. Although a moment may also use N m, it describes turning effect rather than energy transfer.','State the relation between the joule and newton metre for work.','1 J = 1 N m.')
add('2.10.4-05','Ideal machines can reduce effort by increasing the distance through which the effort moves.','In a lossless arrangement, input work equals output work.','A lever, gear system or movable pulley can trade a smaller force for a greater effort distance. This does not create energy. Real friction increases the input work needed for a given useful output. A fixed pulley may mainly change force direction without reducing its size; identify the arrangement before making an effort claim.','Explain why a machine can reduce effort without creating energy.','Link the smaller effort to a larger effort distance.~In an ideal machine their product gives the same input work as the useful output work.')
add('2.10.4-05-M01','A lever with a longer effort arm can require less effort for a fixed load moment.','The effort point then moves farther than the load point for the same rotation.','Balance moments to compare force sizes, then compare point movements through the rotation. For an ideal lever, halving effort while doubling its movement leaves work unchanged. A real lever has friction and deformation losses. The longer arm is a moment arm, while actual movement is used for work.','Describe how an ideal lever reduces effort while conserving work.','A longer effort arm reduces the force but makes its point move farther for the same load movement.')
add('2.10.4-05-M02','Meshing gears transfer rotational motion and change turning effect and rotation rate.','A larger driven gear turns more slowly and can provide greater turning effect than a smaller driving gear in an ideal pair.','Each tooth engages corresponding motion at the contact. Adjacent external gears rotate in opposite directions. The increase in turning effect is accompanied by fewer turns over the same time; it does not create power. Real friction reduces useful output. A bicycle’s gear choice changes the force-distance or turning-effect-speed trade.','Describe what happens when a small gear drives a larger external gear in an ideal pair.','The driven gear turns more slowly in the opposite direction and provides a greater turning effect.')
add('2.10.4-05-M03','A movable pulley can share a load between supporting rope sections.','Reducing effort requires pulling a greater rope length in the ideal arrangement.','With two parallel supporting sections carrying equal tension, the ideal effort is half the supported load, while twice the load’s rise must be pulled through the free end. A single fixed pulley changes direction with no ideal force reduction. Pulley mass and friction must be included when they matter.','Describe the force-distance trade for an ideal movable pulley supported by two rope sections.','The effort is half the supported load, but the free rope end moves twice as far as the load.')
add('2.10.4-06','Efficiency is the percentage of total input energy transferred into the intended useful output.','The useful output depends on the purpose of the device.','Heating is useful in a heater but often unwanted in a motor. Define the output before computing the fraction. An efficiency below 100% means some input reaches other stores or surroundings; it does not mean energy is destroyed. Equivalent useful-output-power over input-power ratios can be used for a common steady interval.','Define energy efficiency.','Efficiency is useful output energy divided by total input energy, expressed as a percentage.')
add('2.10.4-07','Percentage efficiency is useful output energy divided by total input energy, multiplied by 100%.','Use matching energy units and include the whole stated input.','If a motor takes in 250 J and gives 175 J of useful mechanical energy, its efficiency is 70%. The remaining 75 J is transferred elsewhere, commonly as internal energy. Reverse the relationship carefully: input energy equals useful output divided by the efficiency expressed as a fraction.','Calculate efficiency for 120 J useful output from 200 J input.','Efficiency = 120/200 × 100%.~The efficiency is 60%.')
add('2.10.5-01','Power is the rate of energy transfer or doing work.','One watt is one joule per second.','Two people can do equal work lifting equal loads to the same height, but the faster lift has greater average power. Power describes how quickly energy transfers, not the total energy transferred. A high-power device operating briefly may use less energy than a lower-power device operating for a long time.','Define power.','Power is the energy transferred or work done per unit time.')
add('2.10.5-02','Average power is P = E/t or W/t over the stated interval.','With energy in joules and time in seconds, power is in watts.','A lift transferring 360 J in 12 s has an average power of 30 W. Rearranging gives E = Pt for constant power. On an energy-time graph, the gradient is power; a straight line means constant rate. If power varies, the whole-interval ratio gives an average rather than each instantaneous value.','Calculate average power for 450 J transferred in 15 s.','P = E/t = 450/15.~The average power is 30 W.')
C=A.choice
C('2.10.3-03-M01','Which store is associated with motion?',('Kinetic','It depends on mass and speed.'),('Gravitational alone','This depends on height.'),('Strain alone','This depends on deformation.'),('Chemical alone','This concerns material configuration.'))
C('2.10.4-01','Which distance belongs in W = Fd for a parallel force?',('Displacement along the force','This is the work distance.'),('Perpendicular arm to a pivot','This belongs to moment.'),('Width of the object','It need not be a displacement.'),('Distance to Earth’s centre','That is not the work distance here.'))
C('2.10.4-06','What does an efficiency below 100% mean?',('Some input is not useful output','Other stores or surroundings receive it.'),('Energy is destroyed','Total energy is conserved.'),('Output exceeds input','That reverses the comparison.'),('Power must be zero','Useful transfer may still occur.'))
C('2.10.5-01','Which unit measures power?',('W','One watt is one joule per second.'),('J','This measures energy.'),('N','This measures force.'),('kg','This measures mass.'))
C('2.10.4-05-M03','What is the main ideal effect of a single fixed pulley?',('Changing effort direction','It does not reduce force in the simple model.'),('Halving effort in that simple arrangement','Two load-supporting sections are not provided.'),('Creating input energy','A machine cannot create energy.'),('Eliminating displacement','Rope and load still move.'))
C('2.10.3-01','Why does a real swinging pendulum slow?',('Energy transfers to surroundings','Drag and friction reduce mechanical energy.'),('Energy disappears','This violates conservation.'),('Gravity vanishes','Gravity continues acting.'),('Mass becomes zero','The object remains present.'))
C('2.10.4-05-M02','A small external gear drives a larger one. How does the larger gear turn?',('Slower and oppositely','Its larger circumference requires more tooth movement per turn.'),('Faster and in the same direction','Both parts of this description are wrong.'),('Slower in the same direction','External contact reverses direction.'),('Without a turning effect','Teeth transmit a turning force.'))
C('2.10.3-04-M02','What is Ek for 2 kg at 3 m/s?',('9 J','Half times 2 times 3 squared is 9.'),('3 J','This fails to square speed.'),('18 J','This omits the half.'),('6 J','This uses mv rather than energy.'))
C('2.10.3-04-M01','What energy is gained by 4 kg raised 0.5 m with g = 10 N/kg?',('20 J','Use mgh.'),('2 J','This omits g.'),('80 J','This divides by height.'),('200 J','This misplaces a factor of ten.'))
C('2.10.4-02','A 5 N force acts along 4 m. What work does it do?',('20 J','Multiply force by displacement.'),('1.25 J','This divides force by distance.'),('0.8 J','This reverses that ratio.'),('9 J','Adding unlike quantities does not give work.'))
C('2.10.4-07','Useful output is 90 J from 150 J input. What is efficiency?',('60%','The useful fraction is 0.6.'),('167%','This reverses the ratio.'),('40%','This is the non-useful fraction.'),('90%','This ignores total input.'))
C('2.10.5-02','What average power transfers 240 J in 8 s?',('30 W','Divide energy by time.'),('1920 W','This multiplies by time.'),('0.033 W','This uses time per energy.'),('240 W','This ignores duration.'))
P=A.part
P('2.10.4-03','State the SI unit of energy.','Joule, J.',1,'One mark for joule.')
P('2.10.5-01','State what 1 W means.','A transfer rate of 1 J/s.',1,'One mark for joule per second.')
P('2.10.4-05','Explain how a lossless machine reduces effort.','The smaller effort acts through a greater displacement. The input and output work are equal.',2,'One mark for greater effort displacement; one for conserved work.')
P('2.10.3-04-M01','Calculate the energy gained by a 3 kg load raised 2 m where g = 10 N/kg.','ΔEp = mgh = 60 J.',2,'One mark for mgh; one for 60 J.')
P('2.10.5-02','Calculate average power when that 60 J lift takes 5 s.','P = E/t = 12 W.',2,'One mark for dividing by time; one for 12 W.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot useful lifting work vertically against elapsed time horizontally for the fixed load lifted at steady speed.',unit='J/s or W',meaning='The work-time gradient is useful lifting power, not the load’s weight. For a steady lift the line has approximately constant gradient.',method=['Select a manageable fixed mass and record its mass.','Measure the starting height and prepare a vertical height scale.','Lift with a low-voltage motor or controlled manual mechanism at approximately steady speed.','Record height gained at several elapsed times and calculate mgh using the stated g.','Repeat the lift and compare work-time trends, keeping clear of suspended loads.'],control='Keep mass and lifting arrangement fixed; avoid acceleration intervals and secure the load against falling.',table='Record time / s, vertical height gained / m and useful work mgh / J.',conclusion='A straight work-time trend supports steady useful power; calculate the gradient and distinguish this useful output from motor input power.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
